<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ivp-second-order" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Initial Value Problems</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    So far we have only been finding the general solution to <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>.
    Just like with first-order ODEs, we can also solve <term>initial value problems</term>.
    However, there is one noticeable difference. With first-order ODEs, we only had one
    constant <m>C</m> to solve for. For example, <m>P(t) = Ce^{2t}</m> solves
    <m>\frac{dP}{dt} = 2P</m>, and knowing <m>P(0)</m> let us solve for <m>C</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now that we have two unknown constants, with a general solution of the form
    <me>y = c_1 y_1 + c_2 y_2,</me>
    knowing just <m>y(0)</m> won't be enough, as we'd have one equation with two unknowns!
    However, if we also know <m>y'(0)</m>, we get a system of two equations:
    <md>
      <mrow>y(0) \amp= c_1 y_1(0) + c_2 y_2(0)</mrow>
      <mrow>y'(0) \amp= c_1 y_1'(0) + c_2 y_2'(0)</mrow>
    </md>
    and we can uniquely solve for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXISTENCE AND UNIQUENESS THEOREM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-existence-uniqueness-second-order">
    <title>Existence and Uniqueness for Second-Order Linear ODEs</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        When solving <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>, there exists a unique solution
        given initial conditions <m>y(0)</m> and <m>y'(0)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    We actually saw this demonstrated earlier in the course in
    <xref ref="ex-position-from-acceleration"/>. When given acceleration
    <m>a(t) = p''(t)</m>, we needed to know both <m>p(0)</m> and <m>v(0) = p'(0)</m>
    to uniquely determine the position function. In general, we see this theme that
    a first-order ODE requires 1 initial condition, and a second-order ODE requires 2.
    This is a theme we'll continue to see as we go.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: IVP
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-ivp-second-order">
    <title>Solving an IVP: <m>y'' - 4y' + 5y = 0</m>, <m>y(0) = 1</m>, <m>y'(0) = 5</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ex-complex-roots"/>, we already found that the general solution
        to <m>y'' - 4y' + 5y = 0</m> is:
        <me>y = e^{2x}\left(c_1\cos(x) + c_2\sin(x)\right).</me>
        Now let's use the initial conditions <m>y(0) = 1</m> and <m>y'(0) = 5</m>
        to find the unique solution.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <hint>
      <p>
        First compute <m>y'</m>. Then plug in <m>x = 0</m> into both <m>y</m> and
        <m>y'</m> to set up the system of equations:
        <md>
          <mrow>1 \amp= y(0) = \ldots</mrow>
          <mrow>5 \amp= y'(0) = \ldots</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Starting with the general solution and computing <m>y'</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>y \amp= e^{2x}(c_1\cos(x) + c_2\sin(x))</mrow>
          <mrow>y' \amp= 2e^{2x}(c_1\cos(x) + c_2\sin(x)) + e^{2x}(-c_1\sin(x) + c_2\cos(x))</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= e^{2x}\left((2c_1 + c_2)\cos(x) + (2c_2 - c_1)\sin(x)\right)</mrow>
        </md>
        Plugging in the initial conditions:
        <md>
          <mrow>1 = y(0) \amp= e^{0}(c_1\cos(0) + c_2\sin(0)) = c_1</mrow>
          <mrow>5 = y'(0) \amp= e^{0}((2c_1 + c_2)\cos(0) + (2c_2 - c_1)\sin(0)) = 2c_1 + c_2</mrow>
        </md>
      From the system above, we get <m>c_1 = 1</m> and <m>c_2 = 3</m>.
      </p>
      
      <solution>
        <title>Algebra for solving the system</title>
        <p>
          We have the system:
          <md>
            <mrow>c_1 \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>2c_1 + c_2 \amp= 5</mrow>
          </md>
          The first equation gives <m>c_1 = 1</m> directly. Substituting into the
          second: <m>2(1) + c_2 = 5</m>, so <m>c_2 = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
      <p>
       Plugging in <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>, we find the unique solution to the IVP is:
        <me>y = e^{2x}\left(\cos(x) + 3\sin(x)\right).</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two initial conditions:</alert> A second-order ODE needs both
          <m>y(0)</m> and <m>y'(0)</m> to uniquely determine a solution, one for
          each unknown constant <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Process:</alert> Find the general solution, compute <m>y'</m>,
          plug in the initial conditions to get a system of two equations, solve
          for <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
